* text=auto
*.rs text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.ts text eol=lf
*.d.ts text eol=lf

# Asset files, and why they need pinning more than the source does.
#
# `bsengine-asset`'s sidecars record a blake3 of the asset's *raw bytes*
# (`measure_file`), and those sidecars are committed. Under `text=auto` alone,
# a Windows checkout gets CRLF and a Linux one gets LF, so the committed hash
# can only ever match one of them -- on the other, every scan finds the asset
# "changed", rewrites the sidecar, and dirties the tree. Pinning eol makes the
# bytes the same everywhere, which is what makes a committed hash meaningful.
*.js text eol=lf
*.ron text eol=lf
*.wgsl text eol=lf
*.meta text eol=lf

# And the ones that must never be touched: a CRLF substitution inside a mesh or
# a sound is silent corruption, not a diff.
*.glb binary
*.gltf binary
*.wav binary
*.ogg binary
*.mp3 binary
*.flac binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ktx2 binary
*.dds binary
